{% renderTemplate "md" %}

The simplest of synthesizer setups just produces a sine wave. Traditionally, this is the waveform used by a [theramin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theremin), hence this example.

In **HYPERBLAM** synthesis, a `<synth-blam>` houses any number of [operators](https://www.etherdiver.com/how-to-synth-a-guide-to-programming-synthesizers/fm-basics-i/#operator). These are either `<carrier-blam>`s or `<modulator-blam>`s. A single one of the first category is all we need here. It produces a wave of `type` sine by default.

```html
<synth-blam id="theramin" notes="C4" detune="0" out="bus">
  <carrier-blam curve="0 0, 1 2" release="2"></carrier-blam>
</synth-blam>
```

Since we want our theramin tone to be continuous, the envelope `curve` starts at zero, moves to one after two beats, and stays there. The `release` value sets how many beats it’ll take for the signal to diminish when **stop** is pressed.

Like with `<note-blam>`, we can supply a comma-separated list of `notes` to choose from. It’s just that these notes determine the frequency of an oscillator rather than the playback speed of a sample.
  
In this case, we are just going to play the note **C4**. The change in pitch you hear (when pressing the **move!** button) is entirely down to automating the synth’s `detune` property.

```html
<tap-blam id="move" to="#theramin" prop="detune" ramp="1" value="-500|1200|2400|300|1500|2700|3600">
  <button>move!</button>
  <blam-blam to="#move" prop="ramp" value="2|1|0.5|0.5"></blam-blam>
  <blam-blam to=".vibrato" prop="gain" ramp="1" value="0|0|0|0|100|200" chance="0.333"></blam-blam>
</tap-blam>
```

Since a theramin’s pitch changes gradually, as you move your hand closer and further away from its sensor, each detuning occurs with a _ramp_. The speed/duration of `ramp` is fluctuated using a `<blam-blam>`. 

The second `<blam-blam>` affects two `<lfo-blam>`s simultaneously. When a gain value above zero is applied, you hear a vibrato effect. The vibrato effect can be simple and steady or complex and turbulent, depending on how _much_ gain is applied to how _many_ LFOs. Attaching multiple parallel LFOs to the same `detune` parameter is the easiest way to create complex oscillation patterns.

```html
<lfo-blam class="vibrato" prop="detune" gain="0" beats="0.21"></lfo-blam>
<lfo-blam class="vibrato" prop="detune" gain="0" beats="0.47"></lfo-blam>
```

All of the above is to emulate the sound of someone _dicking about_ with a theramin. Because expert theramin players are rare but anyone can play one.

The synth is routed through a _bus_ with a nice plate reverb to place the theramin in a large space.

```html 
<chain-blam id="bus">
  <reverb-blam mix="0.25">
    <sample-blam src="{{site.basedir}}/static/sounds/IRs/plate.mp3"></sample-blam>
  </reverb-blam>
</chain-blam>
```

